+
+    /// Whether a condemned worker's weights have actually been returned to MLX.
+    ///
+    /// Conjunctive, and each half covers what the other cannot.
+    ///
+    /// `containerDeallocated` alone is a lifetime proxy that can fire early:
+    /// see ``WeightReleaseObservation/containerDeallocated``. It is kept
+    /// because it is the cheap, exact half — while the wrapper is alive the
+    /// weights are certainly still held, and no byte count should be able to
+    /// out-vote that.
+    ///
+    /// The byte test is the substantive half, and it asks the only authority
+    /// that actually owns the buffers: MLX's own allocator. It is an *exact*
+    /// accounting of this worker's own weights — at least as many bytes as the
+    /// load cost must have stopped being active — not the proportional
+    /// half-baseline crossing review rejected in revision 2. On the 29 GB
+    /// target model this demands all 29 GB back, where a fraction admitted the
+    /// clear with ~14.5 GB still resident.
+    ///
+    /// A `weightFootprintBytes` of zero or less fails closed. That is the
+    /// absence-versus-failure-to-read rule: an unmeasured footprint is not a
+    /// model that cost nothing, and a comparison against it would be satisfied
+    /// by any reading at all. The teardown then abandons — loudly, with the
